00:00:03.110 --> 00:00:05.030 align:start position:0%
a potential turning point for kazakhstan  the president says the referendum will  decentralize the political system and  outlaw nepotism  it comes after peaceful protests over  fuel prices turned deadly  toppling the former president's  three-decade rule  but the proposed reforms have surprised  some and excited others  we were warned that there would be a  referendum literally a month ago and  more than 90 percent of our society do  not know or understand what a referendum  is  the importance of constitutional  amendments is obvious i came to vote for  the first time in my life and i'm 54  years old so for me this referendum is  very important i even wrote a request at  another polling station that they come  to my home so that my father who is 92  could also vote  the changes to kazakhstan's constitution  critics say will only bolster the  president's ambitions for a second term  there's a lot of questions about whether  this will really will reset relations  between the  state and its citizens which are pretty  traumatized after the violence in  january  the president also faces another  challenge staying neutral on the war in  ukraine while maintaining security and  economic partnerships with russia and  the west  julian wolf al jazeera
 

